WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.010 --> 00:00:05.775
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.775 --> 00:00:07.311
Dream it up.

00:00:07.311 --> 00:00:10.630
Dream something bigger than I did.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:16.060
That's what my grandpa always
told me when I was small.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:23.310
Grandpa was born in the late 1920s,
when Vietnam was still a French colony.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:29.520
Life was tough,
starvation was a constant fear.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:36.872
Growing up, all my grandpa
could dream of was survival.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:43.169
My father was born just a year before
the French finally left Vietnam.

00:00:43.169 --> 00:00:48.383
For the first time,
Vietnamese believed the peace and

00:00:48.383 --> 00:00:51.680
independence could be possible.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.930
But then the American came, and
for 21 years of the Vietnam War,

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:02.100
more than 3 million
Vietnamese were killed.

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:07.350
Like grandpa,
my father dreamed of survival.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:12.750
But this time,
he dreamed of something else as well.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:13.987
He dreamed of peace.

00:01:16.312 --> 00:01:22.726
And then me, I was born when neither
the French nor the American were around.

00:01:22.726 --> 00:01:26.521
Food was scarce, but I grew up in peace.

00:01:26.521 --> 00:01:29.657
Unlike many others of my generation,

00:01:29.657 --> 00:01:34.660
I could afford to dream something else,
something bigger.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.520
I dreamed of a good education.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:44.550
Three generations,
three very different dreams.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:48.430
To many people, it is a generational gap.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:51.617
To me, it is a dream gap.

00:01:54.915 --> 00:01:58.820
For most of my life, I've been obsessed
with asking people what their dreams are.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:04.780
And having asked hundreds if not thousands
of people the exact same question,

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:10.180
I know for a fact that your background
has so much to do with your dreams.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:13.240
In fact,
your background can dictate your dreams.

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:19.490
In other words, the time you're born,
the place you grow up in,

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:23.200
the family you come from can
determine what your dreams are,

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.510
can determine where your
starting point in life is.

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:34.468
Which explains why more than 20 million
today are still at risk of starvation,

00:02:34.468 --> 00:02:38.330
still dream of their next
meal every single day.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:43.960
Which explains why millions of people
in countries such as Syria and

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:45.877
Yemen still dream of peace.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:54.560
Which also explains why million of
kids still dream of going to school.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.521
So many years have passed,
but at different places,

00:02:58.521 --> 00:03:04.068
in different circumstances, millions
of these people still have the same

00:03:04.068 --> 00:03:09.367
dreams as three generations of my
family had years ago, the dream gap.

00:03:09.367 --> 00:03:15.645
But my talk today is less
about why the dream gap,

00:03:15.645 --> 00:03:19.179
but what we can do about it.

00:03:19.179 --> 00:03:23.907
I'm going to share with you a story
of a 14 year old boy, Kelvin,

00:03:23.907 --> 00:03:29.330
who lives in a slum outside of Nairobi,
whom I met when I was living in Kenya.

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:35.090
Kelvin sold nuts down
the street where I lived.

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:40.671
He got me really curious, because I saw
him sell nuts and take care of his younger

00:03:40.671 --> 00:03:45.770
siblings who were always by his side,
and sometimes, big bucks, too.

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:50.020
I always bought nuts from Kelvin.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:55.614
But one day I asked him the question,
what's your dream, Kelvin?

00:03:57.644 --> 00:04:00.480
Dream, I have none.

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:08.410
As a wish, though,
I wish I had enough money to buy food for

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:10.550
my five younger brothers and sisters.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:16.110
He went on to tell me about his family.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:20.660
But his father had left him a few
years earlier, his mother was sick.

00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:25.754
And at the age of 14 he was
the breadwinner of his family.

00:04:25.754 --> 00:04:31.919
It's people like Kelvin that
have taught me again and

00:04:31.919 --> 00:04:38.496
again to be curious about
the dreams of people around me.

00:04:38.496 --> 00:04:43.696
As different as we seem to be,
I felt connected to him,

00:04:43.696 --> 00:04:51.171
because in many ways, he has the same
dream as my grandfather did, survival.

00:04:54.808 --> 00:05:01.680
Growing up, I heard the saying, you
are as big as your dream, and I hated it.

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:08.969
In fact, I was afraid that people
would judge me for the dream I had.

00:05:08.969 --> 00:05:14.391
But knowing that your background has so
much to do with your dreams, I would never

00:05:14.391 --> 00:05:19.910
judge Kelvin, or anyone for that matter,
for how big or how small his dreams are.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.620
It's people like Kelvin that
remind me of the dream gap.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:30.832
That we come from different places,
we have different dreams, and that's okay.

00:05:33.586 --> 00:05:37.570
One day, I asked Kelvin,
have you thought of going to college?

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:41.920
He said,
that is something I really want to do.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:49.570
You probably call it a dream, but I'm
not sure if I can make it or afford it.

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:56.810
So I tell him about me,
about my dream and how I pursued it.

00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:01.600
And I told him that I would
help him pursue his dream too.

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:07.672
So every Sunday at 4 PM I would help
Kellvin sell nuts on the streets

00:06:07.672 --> 00:06:08.618
of Nairobi.

00:06:08.618 --> 00:06:13.629
100 packs, $0.05 apiece, $5 was what

00:06:13.629 --> 00:06:19.370
he needed to feed his family and
to afford to go to school.

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:24.355
And in between, I would teach him math,
his favorite subject.

00:06:24.355 --> 00:06:29.146
It's times like this with
Kelvin that made me realize

00:06:29.146 --> 00:06:32.879
that knowing somebody's dream is good.

00:06:32.879 --> 00:06:37.093
But helping them with that dream, and
helping them build on that dream,

00:06:37.093 --> 00:06:38.070
is even better.

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:43.150
And helping isn't necessarily
about giving money, but

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:47.090
it's about giving them support,
giving them a little push.

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:52.130
The day I left Nairobi for
Stanford, it was a Sunday.

00:06:53.618 --> 00:06:59.580
I tell Kelvin, it's my last day selling
nuts with you, I'm leaving tomorrow.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:03.250
He was surprised.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:03.950
Why are you leaving?

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:10.060
I told him, remember my dream,
good education?

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:12.270
I'm still pursuing it.

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:16.034
I'm pursuing my and
you got to pursue yours too.

00:07:18.252 --> 00:07:22.761
You see, the stories of people
like Kelvin or my grandfather or

00:07:22.761 --> 00:07:27.450
father, so the stories of dreams
dictated by their background.

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:31.680
But look around, there are so

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:36.830
many Kelvins still out there in the world,
still facing the dream gap.

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:43.877
I hope that with this story, you will
reach out to the Kelvins around you.

00:07:43.877 --> 00:07:48.591
You'll be curious about their dreams and
their lives and their stories.

00:07:48.591 --> 00:07:53.419
And that you will give them a little
support on their journey to achieve their

00:07:53.419 --> 00:07:54.030
dreams.

00:07:55.870 --> 00:08:02.395
It's my grandfather that told me to dream
it up which has led me here at Stanford.

00:08:02.395 --> 00:08:08.054
Where I found a quote at the G Building,
which says, dedicated to the things

00:08:08.054 --> 00:08:13.370
that have not yet happened, and
the people who are about to dream it up.

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:17.898
If there is a place like this for
me, there must be

00:08:17.898 --> 00:08:22.868
a place like this for
the Kelvins around all of us too,

00:08:22.868 --> 00:08:27.720
thank you
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:29.607
[MUSIC]

